{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## HANDOFF-DF3A: a `/rebar-debug` subagent is root-causing this; do NOT force-close b586 to get around it\n\nState at handoff: reproduced twice, deterministic, fail-closed. A subagent is running the `/rebar-debug`\ntwo-phase protocol against it. No fix attempted yet.\n\n**The one instruction that matters:** the only override this failure offers is `--force-close`, and using\nit is the wrong move. A force-closed ticket is the unattested state epic\n[rebar:e369-a449-4773-48fb] spent its whole cycle removing — it is literally why `b586`, `0059` and\n`2bc7` were reopened. b586 is therefore left `in_progress` with everything else in place: all acceptance\ncriteria checked, a fresh PASS plan-review attestation, and `child_closure_findings` reporting 0 blocking\n/ 0 uncertified. It is one well-formed verdict away from closing.\n\n**Isolate before concluding.** The leading hypothesis is that re-parenting b586's two orphaned children\n(one of which `depends_on` the other) introduced dep/link records into the verifier's assembled context,\nand the structured-output step now returns one of those records instead of a verdict. The id in the bad\npayload is exactly that link's target. That is a strong correlation and NOT an isolated cause — ordinary\nmodel formatting drift on a large context is a live alternative. The first acceptance criterion is to\nreproduce with and without the `depends_on` link present.\n\n**Do not \"fix\" this by loosening the schema** to accept a link record. If the cause is context assembly\nleaking link records into the verdict slot, fix it at the source. Widening `CompletionVerdict` would make\nthe gate accept garbage, which is worse than the current loud refusal — the fail-closed handling here is\ncorrect, the returned SHAPE is the defect.\n\nIf the root cause turns out to be the re-parenting, note that reverting it is not a clean escape: it\nwould re-orphan two closed, certified tickets and re-break b586's acceptance criteria, which asserted the\nparentage. The structure is right; the verifier needs to cope with it."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785878545579139001,"uuid":"a08d2d49-9c8d-4ddd-9015-01ace6384a03"}